# Compiling and publishing a PDF

A PDF build invokes powerful external tools and may require several passes. It can
fail after producing logs, auxiliary files, or even a malformed PDF. None of those
intermediate states should replace the reader's previous publication.

Literate separates compilation from publication. Compilation works in a private
directory and returns owned PDF bytes. Publication then compares and commits those
bytes through the ordinary output transaction.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Plan[Complete TeX and companion plan] --> Stage[Private build directory]
    Stage --> Preflight[Check required tools and packages]
    Preflight --> Build[Run bounded TeX build]
    Build --> Verify[Verify convergence, rail, and PDF bytes]
    Verify --> Bytes[Owned PDF bytes]
    Bytes --> Commit[Transactional destination commit]
```

The arrows show the only route to the destination. Compiler files cannot bypass
verification. The diagram omits diagnostic retention; warnings and an optionally
retained build directory travel beside success or failure without becoming the
published artifact.

## Validate the plan before staging

Compiler inputs use normalized portable relative paths. Empty components, parent
steps, absolute paths, drive syntax, control characters, reserved device names,
and case-folded collisions are rejected. The selected entry must be a TeX file and
must exist in the plan.

Inside the private directory, the entry receives a fixed compiler basename while
companions keep their relative locations. The fixed basename reserves the nearby
compiler-output namespace and prevents an input from colliding with logs,
auxiliaries, or the final private PDF.

Every staged file is created exclusively with restrictive permissions. The private
root is unpredictable and accessible only to its owner. Parent/file conflicts fail
during staging and follow the same cleanup path as later tool failures.

A private directory is containment for outputs, not a sandbox for TeX. TeX can read
files and execute complex language features. Shell escape is disabled as defense in
depth, but the compiler still receives trusted renderer output rather than
untrusted arbitrary TeX.

## Preflight gives actionable failures

Before compiling, the adapter verifies that the build orchestrator, LuaTeX engine,
package lookup tool, document class, and required packages are available. It does
not invoke a package manager or download missing software.

The lookup runs with the same engine context used by the build. This catches the
common case where a package exists for another TeX configuration but is unavailable
to the selected engine. Font availability remains the font system's responsibility
because it depends on selected installed families or packaged companions.

Preflight runs inside the private build after staging. If the caller chooses to
retain failed builds, the exact compiler inputs are therefore available for
inspection.

## Own the entire process tree

A timeout is useful only if it can stop the work that consumed it. The TeX build
orchestrator may launch an engine and other descendants, so terminating only the
direct child could leave compilation running.

On supported systems each invocation receives its own process group. Cancellation,
timeout, output overflow, and normal leader exit all trigger group cleanup. The
runner waits for pipe closure, with a short fallback for descriptors inherited by a
stray descendant. A shell is never involved; commands and arguments are passed as
literal vectors.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Running
    Running --> Draining: leader exits
    Running --> Stopping: timeout, cancellation, or output limit
    Stopping --> Draining: process group terminated
    Draining --> Accepted: clean close and valid status
    Draining --> Rejected: process or stream failure
    Accepted --> [*]
    Rejected --> [*]
```

The state diagram emphasizes that an `exit` event alone is not completion. Output
pipes and descendants still belong to the invocation. It omits staging cleanup,
which surrounds the entire state machine.

Combined standard output and error have one aggregate byte limit. The runner counts
bytes before retaining chunks and continues draining after the limit is crossed.
Stream errors are failures, not unhandled events or permission to accept a partial
transcript.

## Bound time, logs, and products

The build has a fixed deadline and a bounded repeat count. The tool is instructed to
stop on TeX errors, avoid shell escape, and ignore user or project build-control
files that could alter the invocation.

Logs and the final PDF are read through file descriptors after checking for regular,
non-link files. The reader confirms file identity and size, then reads at most the
measured size plus one byte so growth cannot pass unnoticed. A successful process
without a bounded log or recognizable nonempty PDF header is still a failed build.

These checks do not constitute a full PDF parser. They establish the narrower
contract needed by publication: the expected compiler output is a bounded regular
file with the right format signature.

## Multi-pass references must converge

Earlier TeX passes often contain undefined references by design. Only the final log
can say whether labels and page references converged. The compiler therefore parses
final warning records, joining wrapped continuation lines without treating arbitrary
book prose as a warning.

A final unresolved-reference warning, a request for another rerun, or exhaustion of
the repeat limit rejects the PDF. Ordinary warnings such as missing glyphs and
overfull boxes are returned to the publisher rather than silently discarded.

Annotated books add another convergence condition. The annotation rail writes a
bounded stability certificate derived from physical page membership and placement.
A missing, malformed, pending, or error certificate rejects a rail-enabled build
before PDF bytes reach the destination transaction.

`publication PDF build` accepts bytes only after the final log and rail certificate
agree.

```ts : <<publication PDF build>>=
async function buildPdf(runner: PdfProcessRunner, cwd: string,
    env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv, check: () => void): Promise<PdfCompilation> {
    const result = await runner(requestFor('latexmk', [
        '-norc', '-lualatex', '-interaction=nonstopmode', '-halt-on-error',
        '-file-line-error', '-no-shell-escape', '-e', '$max_repeat = 5;', 'book.tex'
    ], cwd, env, buildTimeoutMs));
    if (result.code !== 0) { throw new Error(`latexmk failed (exit ${result.code}).\n${result.output}`); }
    const log = (await readBoundedFile(path.join(cwd, 'book.log'), maximumLogBytes, check)).toString('utf8');
    checkConvergence(log, result.output);
    await checkRailCertificate(cwd, check);
    const pdf = await readBoundedFile(path.join(cwd, 'book.pdf'), maximumPdfBytes, check);
    check();
    if (pdf.length < 8 || !/^%PDF-[12]\.[0-9]/.test(pdf.subarray(0, 8).toString('ascii'))) {
        throw new Error('LuaLaTeX did not produce a valid PDF header in book.pdf');
    }
    return { pdf, warnings: logWarnings(log) };
}
```

## Preserve the previous output

Let `O` be the bytes already published and `N` the newly compiled bytes. The desired
observable rule is:

```text
failed before commit  ⇒  destination remains O
successful commit     ⇒  destination becomes N
```

The private compiler never opens the destination, so staging, preflight, conversion,
and verification cannot damage `O`. After compilation succeeds, the command reduces
the complete plan to one final PDF destination and passes it to the shared output
transaction.

The transaction compares bytes first. Equal bytes remain current and cause no
write. Changed bytes are staged beside the target, the old file is preserved for
rollback, and success is reported only after the batch commits. A committed cleanup
failure is reported honestly as committed; it is not mislabelled as a failed
replacement.

This is recoverable exception safety, not simultaneous filesystem-wide atomicity.
Concurrent external writers remain outside the guarantee.

## Cancellation respects the commit boundary

Cancellation is checked during plan creation, staging, preflight, subprocess work,
file reads, and while rollback remains possible. Once the output transaction has
committed, a late cancellation cannot make those bytes uncommitted.

For that reason, the command does not perform a new cancellation check after the
transaction returns. It reports either success or a committed cleanup error. Signal
listeners remain installed until the coordinator settles so rollback and cleanup
are not abandoned midway.

## Retained builds transfer ownership

Normally the private directory is removed in a `finally` path after diagnostics and
PDF bytes have been captured. Cleanup failure is itself visible; success never
quietly leaves an undisclosed build tree.

An explicit keep-build choice transfers ownership of the directory to the caller on
success or failure after staging. The result reports that path together with
warnings. Validation failures before a directory exists naturally have nothing to
retain.

The returned PDF bytes are copied out of the private stage and remain valid after
normal cleanup. The caller never publishes by moving the compiler's own output
path.

## Honest platform and reproducibility limits

Safe process-tree termination is required for the default compiler adapter. A host
without that capability is rejected before spawning. An alternative runner may be
injected for testing, but it inherits the obligation to honor timeout, output, and
completion contracts.

A reproducible source snapshot and stable plan do not by themselves promise
byte-identical PDF files. TeX installations, fonts, metadata, and external tools can
affect bytes. When a reproducible-build timestamp is supplied, the environment is
preserved rather than invented, but the publication contract is semantic success
and safe replacement, not universal binary reproducibility.

The command-line path now has clear ownership from snapshot to final transaction.
The final chapter in this part explains why the editor does not run that machinery
inside its own process and how a bounded external helper preserves trust,
cancellation, and fresh-buffer semantics.
